:"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $|f_n(x)-f(x)|\\leq \\frac{1}{n}$ for all $x\\in X$, what can be concluded?","options":["Only pointwise convergence can be concluded.","$f_n\\rightrightarrows f$ on $X$.","$f_n$ cannot converge uniformly because the bound depends on $n$.","$f$ must be the zero function."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2e18ba69dac9be781dbc8737","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_uniform_convergence_1e86697e_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What distinguishes uniform convergence of functions on a common domain from pointwise convergence?","options":["For each $\\varepsilon>0$, one threshold works at all points.","For each $\\varepsilon>0$, a separate threshold at each point suffices.","The error must eventually be exactly zero at every point.","The domain must be a bounded interval of real numbers."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"852c8aa06ad8ca4f5eb75596","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_uniform_convergence_1e86697e_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $f_n\\rightrightarrows f$ and each $f_n$ is continuous, which preservation result is relevant?","options":["Uniform convergence destroys continuity.","Uniform convergence implies each $f_n$ is constant.","The uniform limit theorem preserves continuity.","The pointwise limit theorem preserves differentiability without conditions."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4e587a3b74aab02b94898cd4","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_uniform_convergence_1e86697e_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which statement describes a common boundary between pointwise and uniform convergence?","options":["If the required $N$ is independent of $x$, the convergence cannot possibly be pointwise convergence.","Pointwise convergence is stronger than uniform convergence.","Uniform convergence requires no limiting function.","If the required $N$ depends on $x$, the argument shows pointwise convergence rather than uniform convergence."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"11bda8cd970f405066aa7fe4","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_uniform_convergence_1e86697e_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which conclusion is too strong to draw from pointwise convergence alone?","options":["That each fixed $x$ has $f_n(x)\\to f(x)$.","That convergence is uniform on $X$.","That there is a limiting function point by point.","That for each fixed point, the values eventually get close to the limit."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"644d654a9839cfce9867e282","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_uniform_convergence_1e86697e_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Suppose $|f_n(x)-f(x)|\\leq \\frac{2}{n+1}$ for every $x\\in X$. For $\\varepsilon=0.1$, what value of $N$ is sufficient for uniform convergence?","options":["$N = 1$","$N = 5$","$N = 10$","$N = 20$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4bab2f3ef9fc71483dd482b2","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_uniform_convergence_1e86697e_012","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"A student proves that for each fixed $x\\in X$ and each $\\varepsilon \\gt 0$ there is an $N_x$ with $|f_n(x)-f(x)| \\lt \\varepsilon$ for $n \\gt N_x$. What is missing for uniform convergence?","options":["One integer $N$ that works for all $x\\in X$.","A proof that $f$ is differentiable.","A proof that $X$ is uncountable.","A proof that $f_n(x) = f(x)$ holds exactly for every $n$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4fcf0bf0b2bf3330461eb66f","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_uniform_convergence_1e86697e_015","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which convergence is implied by uniform convergence?","options":["Divergence at every point","Only convergence at one point","Failure of boundedness","Pointwise convergence"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ede168ea364e320384802271","time_limit":20,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_uniform_convergence_1e86697e_016","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Suppose that for every $\\varepsilon>0$ there exists an integer $N$ such that $|f_n(x)-f(x)|<\\varepsilon$ for every $x\\in X$ and every $n\\ge N$. What convergence does this define?","options":["Uniform convergence","Only convergence at one point","Divergence","Compactness"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"56612d42461764eddb6a8fc8","time_limit":25,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_uniform_convergence_1e86697e_018","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $f_n\\rightrightarrows f$ and every $f_n$ is continuous under the uniform limit theorem, what property can pass to $f$?","opt